WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.497
In the morning, Heidi heard a familiar whistle.

2
00:00:03.498 --> 00:00:08.962
She ran outside, and there were the goats, leaping about with Peter in their midst.

3
00:00:08.963 --> 00:00:11.774
"Hello, Daisy! Hello, Dusky!

4
00:00:11.775 --> 00:00:13.676
Do you remember me?"

5
00:00:13.677 --> 00:00:17.959
she said as Grandfather led his two goats from their pen.

6
00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:22.639
The goats recognized Heidi's voice and came up to rub their heads against her.

7
00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:27.455
Heidi was out of her mind with delight to be back with her old friends again.

8
00:00:27.456 --> 00:00:31.034
"Good morning, Peter," Heidi called out.

9
00:00:31.035 --> 00:00:33.458
"Will you come up the mountain with me?"

10
00:00:33.459 --> 00:00:36.787
Peter asked, as if Heidi had never gone away.

11
00:00:36.788 --> 00:00:43.017
"Tomorrow, perhaps," said Heidi. "I promised to visit Grannie today."

12
00:00:43.018 --> 00:00:45.154
"I am glad you are back!"

13
00:00:45.155 --> 00:00:49.329
said Peter, and his whole face beamed with pleasure.

14
00:00:49.330 --> 00:00:54.726
Heidi spent the rest of the morning helping Grandfather with chores around the hut.

15
00:00:54.727 --> 00:01:00.038
In the afternoon, she went down to visit Grannie, who met her at the door.

16
00:01:00.039 --> 00:01:03.522
"Is it you, Heidi?" said Grannie.

17
00:01:03.523 --> 00:01:07.450
Then, she took Heidi's hand and held it in her own.

18
00:01:07.451 --> 00:01:12.921
As they went inside, Heidi told Grannie that she had a surprise for her.

19
00:01:12.922 --> 00:01:18.855
Grannie sat with a blanket across her knees, and Heidi went over to a small bookshelf.

20
00:01:18.856 --> 00:01:24.696
She took down one of Grannie's prayer books, held the book open, and began to read.

21
00:01:24.697 --> 00:01:28.462
A look of unbelievable joy filled Grannie's face.

22
00:01:28.463 --> 00:01:32.057
Heidi had never seen her so happy before.

23
00:01:32.058 --> 00:01:36.242
Heidi sat down beside her and finished reading the prayer.

24
00:01:36.243 --> 00:01:40.309
"Please, read it again," said Grannie.

25
00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:44.721
After Heidi finished it a second time, Grannie said,

26
00:01:44.722 --> 00:01:49.200
"Ah, Heidi, that brings light to my heart!

27
00:01:49.201 --> 00:01:54.230
What comfort you have brought me!"

28
00:01:54.231 --> 00:01:58.147
Heidi asked Grannie if she had enjoyed the white rolls.

29
00:01:58.148 --> 00:02:02.748
Grannie had eaten two the day before, and another that morning.

30
00:02:02.749 --> 00:02:06.454
"They are wonderful!" she said.

31
00:02:06.455 --> 00:02:10.871
"I do believe they will make me feel stronger."

32
00:02:10.872 --> 00:02:13.098
Heidi was thrilled.

33
00:02:13.099 --> 00:02:20.461
"Clara's father has given me some money, and the baker in Dorfli makes white rolls," she said.

34
00:02:20.462 --> 00:02:26.006
"I am going to buy you white rolls every day and two on Sundays!"

35
00:02:26.007 --> 00:02:31.967
"No, no, Heidi, that money is for you," said Grannie.

36
00:02:31.968 --> 00:02:35.354
But Heidi would not take no for an answer.

37
00:02:35.355 --> 00:02:45.137
Heidi passed the day reading to Grannie, enjoying it as much as she enjoyed playing with the goats or running among the flowers on the mountain.

38
00:02:45.138 --> 00:02:49.957
When she returned home, she told Grandfather all about her day.

39
00:02:49.958 --> 00:02:52.952
Heidi danced for joy around the hut.

40
00:02:52.953 --> 00:02:58.196
"Oh, Grandfather, everything is happier now than it was before!

41
00:02:58.197 --> 00:03:08.748
If God had let me come home earlier, I would not have been able to read to Grannie, and she likes my reading so much."

42
00:03:08.749 --> 00:03:12.146
She stopped her dancing and looked at Grandfather.

43
00:03:12.147 --> 00:03:16.249
"God arranged it all so much better than I could have.

44
00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:20.297
So we will pray every day and never forget Him!

45
00:03:20.298 --> 00:03:22.417
Won't we, Grandfather?"

46
00:03:22.418 --> 00:03:28.463
"And what if one does forget God?" asked Grandfather in a low voice.

47
00:03:28.464 --> 00:03:31.564
"Then everything goes wrong.

48
00:03:31.565 --> 00:03:40.006
If you run away from God when He could have helped you, then He leaves you to yourself," said Heidi seriously.

49
00:03:40.007 --> 00:03:45.567
"That is true," said Grandfather. "Where did you learn that?"

50
00:03:45.568 --> 00:03:50.191
"Grandmamma explained it all to me," said Heidi.

51
00:03:50.192 --> 00:03:54.751
"And a man who God forgets is forgotten forever,"

52
00:03:54.752 --> 00:04:00.984
said Grandfather, almost as if he were thinking of something that happened long ago.

53
00:04:00.985 --> 00:04:05.197
"Oh no, Grandfather, we can always go back.

54
00:04:05.198 --> 00:04:07.340
Grandmamma said so.

55
00:04:07.341 --> 00:04:10.458
God is always watching over us.

56
00:04:10.459 --> 00:04:16.623
All we need to do is start praying, and He will answer our prayers when it is time."

57
00:04:16.624 --> 00:04:21.983
As Heidi lay sleeping that night, Grandfather climbed up to the hay loft.

58
00:04:21.984 --> 00:04:31.846
He knelt beside Heidi's bed and, for the first time in many long years, he prayed.

